.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- execution-target device for gpu(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/gpuclone
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I gpu
device is the native backend
.IR gpu (2)'s
own
.B Backend.apply
talks to when configured for
.BR "device gpu" ;
scripts and most Limbo programs should reach it through
.IR gpu (2)
rather than opening it directly.
.PP
Unlike
.IR ssl (3),
.I gpu
binds flat into
.B /dev
(matching
.IR audio (3)'s
own
.BR /dev/audio / /dev/audioctl
convention, not
.IR ssl (3)'s
own numbered-subdirectory one) \(em its one file is
.BR /dev/gpuclone ,
not a
.B clone
file under a
.B /dev/gpu
directory. Opening
.B /dev/gpuclone
allocates one handle: matrix state (the uploaded
.IR sparse (2)
CSR arrays) and the pending response live on that one open channel,
which also serves both reads and writes \(em there is no separate
.B ctl
/
.B data
split, since every message on a handle is naturally one request
immediately followed by one response.
.PP
The protocol is line-oriented text, not binary, for now (see
.B PERFORMANCE
below). Each write is one request; the following read returns its
response. Two request shapes, both starting with a one-letter tag:
.EX
	U n nnz
	rowptr (n+1 space-separated integers)
	colidx (nnz space-separated integers)
	val (nnz space-separated reals)
.EE
Uploads an
.IR n \(mu n
CSR matrix with
.I nnz
nonzeros, replacing any previous upload on this handle. The following
read returns
.B OK
on success, or an error.
.EX
	X x (n space-separated reals)
.EE
Computes
.IR y = Ax
against the uploaded matrix (an error if none has been uploaded, or
.I x
doesn't have exactly
.I n
values). The following read returns
.I y
the same way, space-separated reals.
.PP
Closing the handle frees its matrix state.
.SH PERFORMANCE
The matvec itself
.RI ( gpuspmv ()
in the source) runs on the CPU, in this device, today \(em despite the
name, nothing here dispatches to an actual GPU yet. This is a
deliberate staging choice, not the finished design: it validates the
whole protocol and the
.IR gpu (2)/ fem (2)
plumbing above it end to end (confirmed:
.IR gpu (2)'s
own
.B available
genuinely returns true through this device, and a
.B "backend gpu"
.IR fem (2)
solve matches an equivalent
.B "backend cpu"
solve to floating-point precision) before adding the actual
acceleration. A real GPU-backed replacement changes only
.B gpuspmv()
and the object this device links against \(em nothing in
.IR gpu (2)'s
own interface, or in this device's own read/write protocol, needs to
change for it \(em mirroring how the existing GPU
.I draw
hooks
.RI ( gpudrawfillpoly3d / g ,
implemented in
.B win-cocoa.m )
already sit one layer below
.IR draw (2)
without
.I draw.m
itself knowing Metal exists. The natural next step is a
.B win-gpu.m
building a real
.B MTLComputePipelineState
kernel, registered in this platform's device manifest
.RB ( emu-cocoa )
the same way
.B draw
already names
.B win-cocoa
as its own extra source.
.PP
The text protocol itself is also a correctness-first choice: real
matrix upload happens once per solve, so parsing overhead doesn't
matter there; a high-throughput version would move the
.B X
exchange \(em the one part called every Krylov iteration \(em to a
compact binary encoding once real GPU compute makes that exchange the
actual bottleneck.
.SH SOURCE
.B /emu/port/devgpu.c
